WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.224
Linux separates normal users from administrative authority.

2
00:00:03.224 --> 00:00:10.593
A normal user can run applications, create personal files, and perform work allowed by that account.

3
00:00:10.593 --> 00:00:12.896
The root account is different.

4
00:00:12.896 --> 00:00:21.647
Root can modify system configuration, install packages, change ownership, stop services, read many protected files, and affect other users.

5
00:00:21.647 --> 00:00:25.332
That makes root powerful, but also high impact.

6
00:00:25.332 --> 00:00:32.701
Mistakes made with root privileges can break systems, expose data, or remove evidence needed for troubleshooting.

7
00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:40.531
Sudo is a controlled way to grant administrative capability without having everyone sign in directly as root.

8
00:00:40.531 --> 00:00:51.585
In a well-managed system, sudo access is intentional: who has it, why they have it, what they are allowed to do, and how that

9
00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:52.967
access is reviewed.

10
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:57.572
Broad sudo access may be convenient, but it creates risk.

11
00:00:57.572 --> 00:01:04.942
It can also hide accountability if shared administrator accounts are used instead of unique user accounts.

12
00:01:04.942 --> 00:01:06.784
Groups help organize access.

13
00:01:06.784 --> 00:01:13.693
A group may control access to files, devices, application directories, operational functions, or administrative capabilities.

14
00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:17.838
Group membership should be treated like permission, not decoration.

15
00:01:17.838 --> 00:01:25.207
Privileged groups deserve periodic review, especially after role changes, contractor offboarding, migrations, or emergency access exceptions.

16
00:01:25.207 --> 00:01:27.510
Service accounts also need care.

17
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:33.037
A service account should have an owner, purpose, authentication method, and lifecycle.

18
00:01:33.037 --> 00:01:39.025
It should not be a forgotten account with broad access and no review.

19
00:01:39.025 --> 00:01:50.079
Good Linux security starts with identity hygiene: unique accounts, limited privilege, strong authentication, documented exceptions, and regular validation that users, groups, sudo rights, and

20
00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:53.303
service accounts still match the system's purpose.
